 "finding": "Across 8 complete windows and 14,237 quotes, 359 groups of records carry the SAME NANOSECOND stamp and 329 of those disagree on price or size. Zero groups were distinct nanosecond stamps aliased together by float seconds, so these are real SIP records and not timestamp rounding. A one-record fetch cannot see them.",
 "power_limitation": "The 24-instant sparse-vs-complete comparison agreed 24 times, but roughly 4.6 percent of quotes participate in a disagreeing same-nanosecond group, so 24 samples had too little power to detect a difference. Agreement there is not evidence of equivalence.",
 "question": "Does a single-record fetch reproduce what the reader would do with the complete window?",
 "resolution": "The collector now retains the COMPLETE FINAL EVENT-TIME GROUP of each window rather than one record. quote_at selects the latest available quote and refuses on conflicts at an event time at or after it, so earlier records cannot change its verdict and every record that can is retained. The equivalence is structural, and is also asserted in tests.",
